{
  "name": "OPS.SLK-C Team Departure",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## OPS.SLK-C · Team Departure\n\nPOST `/webhook/team-departure` `{\"email\": \"...\"}` with header `x-departure-token: <SLACK_DEPARTURE_TOKEN>` → resolve the same channels B would have used → (live only) `conversations.kick` from each → row `departed` → `#ops-alerts`. Default channels (#announcements, #general) are skipped — Slack refuses to remove anyone from them.\n\n**Fail-closed auth.** The webhook is public, and kicking people is destructive, so it requires the token header. `SLACK_DEPARTURE_TOKEN` unset = every call gets 401. Set it before activating. (A Supabase DB webhook on status → departed can call this later.)\n\n**Per-rep channels are gone (2026-09-14).** There is no private channel to archive or deregister any more; removing the person from `#sales-<market>` and the other role channels is the whole job.\n\n**No Slack id on the row** (never joined) → the row is still marked departed so a later stray join is refused by B, and #ops-alerts says nothing was removed.\n\n**Shadow mode** (`SLACK_ONBOARDING_MODE` ≠ live) evaluates everything and posts one `[SHADOW]` line; no kicks, no DB update.\n\n**Auth (2026-09-09):** Slack calls use the n8n credential **Reece Bot** (slackApi) and Supabase calls the credential **LP Supabase** (supabaseApi) — same as A and B. `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` / `LP_SUPABASE_KEY` env vars are not used here.\n\n`Resolve Channels` embeds `scripts/lib/slack-onboarding-resolve.js` verbatim — the test suite checks it.",
        "height": 500,
        "width": 3076,
        "color": 4
      },
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -64,
        -560
      ],
      "id": "df8fae50-6bb8-4ef2-acec-2bb6a280fbbd"
    },
